{% from "macros.j2" import q, plain with context %}
<section class="panel" id="tab-repurposing" data-name="repurposing">
{{ plain('repurposing') }}
{% set RS = SL.rp_summary %}
<h2>Repurposing: existing inhibitors never tried in UC {{ q('repurposing') }}</h2>
<p class="lede">The Validation shortlist asks for targets with no drug at all. This page asks the other question: which genes that rise in UC as a change of the tissue already have an <strong>inhibitor</strong> in the clinic for another disease, never tried in UC, Crohn's disease or IBD? There are {{ SL.rp | length }}, {{ RS.approved_inhibitor }} of them with an approved inhibitor. A molecule with known human pharmacology exists, so testing it is faster; it still needs the same proof as a new drug, that blocking the target helps rather than harms the inflamed mucosa.</p>
<div class="sl-legend"><span><i class="lt green"></i> meets</span><span><i class="lt amber"></i> partly</span><span><i class="lt red"></i> fails</span><span><i class="lt grey"></i> not measured</span></div>
<div class="howbox"><h4>How this list differs from the Validation shortlist</h4><ul>
<li>The same lights, points and groups (stable in the top ten under six scorings), with two lights read differently.</li>
<li><strong>Inhibitor (stage)</strong> replaces "druggable": green when the inhibitor is approved, amber in phase 2 or 3 (or an approval known only from an ATC code), red earlier.</li>
<li><strong>Safety</strong> adds a flag when the inhibitor was made only for cancer ({{ RS.cancer_drug | join(", ") }}): a dose and toxicity chosen for cancer may not suit a chronic disease. Here a drug's known side effects are real information, not just a sign that the gene is studied.</li>
<li><strong>Immune checkpoints</strong> ({{ RS.checkpoints | join(", ") }}) are red on both: they rise in UC as brakes on inflammation, and their blockers cause colitis in patients, so blocking them is the wrong direction.</li>
<li><strong>UC literature</strong> comes from the same review as the shortlist, run on these genes: the most cited papers that name the gene in their title or abstract with colitis or IBD, each call backed by a sentence copied from the paper. Green: blocking or removing the gene helped in colitis; red: the gene protects (its loss worsened colitis), so an inhibitor may harm{% if RS.literature_protective %} ({{ RS.literature_protective | join(", ") }}){% endif %}. This is what tells, for a well-studied gene, whether the inhibitor goes in the right direction.</li>
<li>Genes made mainly by neutrophils ({{ RS.neutrophil_directed | join(", ") }}) are read as on the shortlist: their per-cell change is measured inside neutrophils.</li></ul></div>
<div class="tw"><table class="sl"><thead><tr><th>Group</th><th>Stable in top 10</th><th>Gene</th><th>Cell family</th><th class="num">Meta log2FC</th><th class="num">UC papers</th>
{% for k, label in SL.rp_criteria %}<th>{{ label }}</th>{% endfor %}<th>Evidence</th></tr></thead><tbody>
{% for r in SL.rp %}<tr{% if r.evidence_ids %} class="claim" data-evidence="{{ r.evidence_ids | evattr }}"{% endif %}><td><strong>{{ r.group }}</strong></td><td title="Position range across the six scorings: {{ r.best_rank }} to {{ r.worst_rank }}">{{ r.stability }} of 6</td><td><strong>{{ r.gene }}</strong></td><td>{{ r.lineage }}</td><td class="num">{{ r.meta_log2fc }}</td><td class="num">{{ r.uc_papers }}</td>
{% for k, label in SL.rp_criteria %}{% set v = r.criteria[k] %}<td class="c"><i class="lt {{ v[0] }}" title="{{ label }}: {{ v[1] }}" aria-label="{{ label }}: {{ v[0] }}, {{ v[1] }}"></i></td>{% endfor %}
<td>{{ r.evidence_ids | cite | safe }}</td></tr>{% endfor %}
</tbody></table></div>
<p class="small">Hover over a light for its values. The inhibitor, its stage and its indication are in each card below.</p>

<h3>Groups A and B, with the proposed first test</h3>
<p class="small">The first test uses the existing inhibitor in the cell system that holds most of the gene's RNA in inflamed single cells, then on explants of inflamed UC mucosa, with a dose compared against its approved or trial indication.</p>
<div class="grid">
{% for r in SL.rp if r.group != "C" %}
<div class="card sl-card"><h3>Group {{ r.group }}: {{ r.gene }} <span class="small">{{ r.lineage }}, in the top 10 in {{ r.stability }} of 6 scorings</span></h3>
<dl>{% for k, label in SL.rp_criteria %}{% set v = r.criteria[k] %}<dt><i class="lt {{ v[0] }}"></i> {{ label }}</dt><dd>{{ v[1] }}</dd>{% endfor %}</dl>
{% if r.papers %}<p class="small"><strong>Key papers:</strong></p><ul class="small">{% for p in r.papers %}<li>{% if p.pmid %}<a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/{{ p.pmid }}/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">PMID {{ p.pmid }}</a>{% else %}{{ p.pmcid }}{% endif %}{% if p.year %} ({{ p.year }}){% endif %}, {{ p.kind | replace('_', ' ') }}{% if p.direction in ('pathogenic', 'protective') %}, <strong>{{ p.direction }}</strong>{% endif %}: {{ p.summary }}</li>{% endfor %}</ul>{% endif %}
<p class="small"><strong>Proposed:</strong> {{ r.experiment }}</p></div>
{% endfor %}
</div>
<div class="note"><strong>How to read this.</strong> These are leads for repurposing, ranked by the same data as the new targets. None has been tested in UC; an inhibitor made for another disease may need another dose, another route or another formulation for the colon, and drugs that kill cells carrying the protein (antibody-drug conjugates) are not on this list.</div>
</section>
